 <h1>Exercise Name:  Seated Dumbbell Wrist Curl (Underhand / Wrist Flexor Curl)</h1>
<u><b><h4>Overview Exercise</h4></b></u>
 
The exercise shown in the video is the Seated Dumbbell Wrist Curl (also called Dumbbell Wrist Flexion). It is an isolation movement where you sit on a bench or chair, rest your forearms on your thighs or a flat surface with your palms facing up, and curl the weight upward using only your wrists.

As highlighted in the clip, this movement specifically targets the forearm flexors (the inner/underside muscles of the forearms).

</div></div>

 <h1>How to Perform (Step-by-Step Guide)</h1>

<u><b><h4>1. Starting Position:</h4></b></u>
 Sit comfortably on a bench or chair with your feet flat on the floor.
 Hold a dumbbell (or a single dumbbell held with both hands, as shown in the video) with an underhand grip (palms facing upward).
 Rest your forearms flat against your thighs so that your wrists extend slightly past your knees.


<u><b><h4>2. The Lowering Phase (Eccentric):</h4></b></u>
 Inhale and slowly lower the dumbbell toward the floor by extending your wrists downward as far as comfortably possible.
 Optional (Advanced): Allow the weight to roll slightly toward your fingertips for a deeper stretch.


<u><b><h4>3. The Lifting Phase (Concentric):</h4></b></u>
 Exhale and curl your wrists upward toward your body by contracting your forearm flexor muscles.
 Keep your forearms stationary against your thighs throughout—do not use your arms or momentum to pull the weight up.


<u><b><h4>4. Pause & Repeat:</h4></b></u>
 Squeeze your forearms tightly at the top position for 1 second, then lower back down in a slow, controlled motion.





 <h1> Key Benefits  </h1>

 
 <b>◉Targeted Forearm Growth:</b> Isolates the wrist flexors to build thick, well-defined lower arm mass.<br>
 
 <b>◉Improved Grip Strength:</b> Enhances your ability to squeeze and hold heavier weights in exercises like deadlifts, pull-ups, and rows.<br>
 
 <b>◉Increased Wrist Stability: </b>Strengthens the connective tissues around the wrist, helping reduce the risk of injury during heavy lifting.<br>
 
 <b>◉Better Athletic Performance: </b>Helps in sports that require strong wrist control and grip, such as rock climbing, tennis, and martial arts.



 <h1>Recommended Sets & Repetitions</h1>

Since the forearms consist of endurance-oriented muscle fibers, they respond best to higher repetitions and controlled time-under-tension rather than excessively heavy weights.

 Sets: 3 to 4 sets
 Reps: 12 to 20 repetitions per set
 Rest: 45 to 60 seconds between sets
 Frequency: 2 to 3 times a week (typically at the end of an upper body or arm workout)


